#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# dev/stabilize-canary — the setup of THE SMALL CANARY of the stabilization workflow's
# `test` stage (DECISIONS.md -> 2026-10-07, "The small canary's setup"; the second repair
# plan's task K7 and its section 6, "The small canary",
# completions/artifacts/M55/stabilization-build/re-review-test-half/plan.md).
#
# No `test` stage has run under the Workflow runtime with real agents. The small canary
# is that run, once, before the real run is opened: ONE invocation of the stage, from its
# first step to its pushed record, and then one more to see what an invocation answers
# that has nothing to do. This tool builds the place it runs in, where NOTHING OF IT CAN
# REACH THIS REPOSITORY OR ITS REMOTE, and prints — as one line — what is then invoked.
#
# WHAT A SETUP MAKES, all of it under ONE root outside the repository:
#
#   ROOT/origin.git    a bare repository: THE CLONE'S ONE REMOTE
#   ROOT/clone         a repository of its own that holds the commit named and the
#                      previous release's commit, fetched from this one BY PATH — so no
#                      remote of it ever named this repository, and none names this
#                      repository's remote. Checked out: the loop branch `fix/RUN`, which
#                      exists there and nowhere else, with ONE commit on the commit named —
#                      under --parts, two: the first WITHHOLDS THE ANSWER KEY in the clone
#                      (THE ANSWER KEY, below) — and on it:
#   ROOT/clone/completions/artifacts/RUN/
#                      THE SYNTHETIC OPENING (below), written through the clone's own
#                      dev/stabilize-record, committed, and pushed to ROOT/origin.git by
#                      the clone's own dev/stabilize-step — vetted, as every push of a
#                      run is
#   ROOT/scratch       the scratch root the invocation is handed
#   ROOT/results       where the session that invokes the stage writes what each
#                      invocation returned (`returns`, below) — whoever starts it
#   ROOT/canary.json   the line this setup printed, kept: what `check` reads the run from
#   ROOT/prompts       under --parts only: ONE PROMPT FILE FOR EACH ACT of a run in parts
#                      (THE ACTS OF A RUN IN PARTS, below)
#
# Usage — every act prints ONE line of JSON on stdout, whatever it exits with:
#
#   dev/stabilize-canary setup --root ROOT --commit REV --run RUN --previous VERSION
#                        --previous-commit SHA [--items ID,ID]
#                        [--parts [--budget N] [--order CLASS,CLASS]]
#       ROOT is an ABSOLUTE path of plain segments (the harness takes no other scratch
#       root) — never a relative one, which would be read from wherever the caller
#       stands. It is not there yet and its parent is, or it is an empty directory, as
#       `mktemp -d` mints one: a root that holds anything is refused (exists), a link
#       planted in it too, so a root is used once. It lies OUTSIDE THE REPOSITORY, judged
#       on its real path: in no working tree of it, the main one's git directory
#       included (inside). REV is the candidate: any name this repository resolves to a
#       commit (no-commit) that holds the two scripts of a run and the harness (unfit) —
#       the COMMITTED bytes, never the working tree. RUN is a slug that this repository does
#       not hold: no completions/artifacts/RUN in the tree or in the commit, and no
#       branch `fix/RUN` here or on a remote (taken) — so a stage launched from the wrong
#       directory finds no such run and halts at its first step with nothing written.
#       VERSION and SHA are the previous release and the commit it was released from (a
#       full sha this repository holds): what the preflight builds the second binary
#       from. --items names the opening's items (THE SYNTHETIC OPENING); without it, the
#       five that are marked there. --parts is THE SWITCH: with it the setup writes
#       THE OPENING OF A RUN IN PARTS (below) in place of the synthetic opening — a run
#       with a queue — and --items names items of THAT opening's table; --budget and
#       --order are that opening's, each with its default there, and neither is taken
#       without the switch (usage); a budget is at most 999991, so that the two raises
#       a prompt file is written for are numbers the harness takes. The commit named
#       must then also hold, in its DECISIONS.md, exactly one entry that states the exit
#       rule (unfit), and the write guard, its fence and its probe (unfit) — AND IT MUST
#       HOLD THIS TOOL AS IT RUNS (unfit): the file that runs
#       is the working tree's, the clone holds the commit's, and a setup under the switch
#       says `ready` only where the two are one file. Asked before anything is made; the
#       refusal says which commit to name — the one checked out, where that holds the
#       file — or that the file is in no commit. So REV is THE TIP OF THE BRANCH WHEN THE
#       SETUP IS RUN: the commit that holds the tool with every repair made since it was
#       built. Nothing else of the commit has to be the working tree's: the record
#       script and the step tool are called by their paths in the clone, the harness is
#       named to every prompt file by its path in the clone, and a session that stands
#       in the clone loads the clone's definitions and settings.
#       Everything is checked before anything is made; a setup that fails after that
#       leaves a root that holds no canary.json, which no act takes for a canary. It ends
#       with `check`, and with the state a fresh run has — `next` is `test`, round 1,
#       attempt 1, the opening owing nothing — or it refuses (not-fresh).
#       Prints {status: ready, root, clone, origin, scratch, results, run, loop, run_dir,
#       candidate, head, remote_head, previous: {version, commit}, door, items: [{item,
#       kind}], seeded, state: {next, round, attempt}, session_cwd, script_path, args,
#       args_then, returns: {first, then}, check, expect: {agents, tool_steps,
#       wall_minutes}} — WHAT THE ORCHESTRATOR INVOKES, below. UNDER --parts the line
#       holds no `seeded`, `args`, `args_then`, `returns` or `expect` — those are the
#       small canary's one stage — and holds instead, after `items`: plants: [{key,
#       class}], withheld — the paths the clone holds withheld (THE ANSWER KEY) —
#       condition, budget, hand_on, order; and, after `state`: queue — the keys
#       the run's queue ranks, first to last — and listed: {WHY: [key]}, every row that
#       is in no queue under the word that keeps it out, as the run's own state says
#       both; and, after `script_path`: prompts — the directory of the prompt files —
#       acts: [{act, args, prompt, return, expect [, human] [, verdicts]}], one for each
#       act of the parts, in the order they are made (THE ACTS OF A RUN IN PARTS) —
#       rulings: [{ruling, args, prompt, return, when}], apart from the acts: THE RULINGS
#       OFF THE MAIN LINE (below), each with a prompt file under a name no act has —
#       guard: {act, script, args, prompt, return, expect, human, snapshot}: THE WRITE
#       GUARD'S PROBE (below), act `part-0`, made first, with its own prompt file and the
#       clone's snapshot it is held to — and
#       tool: {sha256, as_committed}: the sha256 of THE FILE THAT RAN, and that it is the
#       file the commit named holds at this tool's path — the one the clone holds, whose
#       header an agent there can read. `as_committed` is true in every line that says
#       `ready`: a setup whose tool is another is refused.
#
#   dev/stabilize-canary check --root ROOT
#       THE CLONE CAN REACH NOTHING BUT ITS BARE REMOTE — asserted, before the session
#       starts and whenever it is asked — and what the run holds, read. ROOT holds a
#       canary (missing), and each of its directories is that directory and no link
#       (link). The clone has ONE remote, `origin`; what git answers that it fetches from
#       and pushes to — under the configuration THE CALLER runs with, so a rewrite in the
#       session's own configuration is seen — is ROOT/origin.git; and the clone's own
#       configuration holds no `url.*` rewrite and names, anywhere in it, no remote of
#       this repository and not this repository (not-isolated). IT WRITES NOTHING, AND
#       TAKES NO LOCK A STAGE COULD MEET: every git command it runs is a read, and its
#       `git status` is asked with no optional lock — a plain one may hold the index's
#       lock for a moment, and a commit step of a stage that stages at that moment is
#       refused and costs its record one more gate.
#       Prints {status: checked, root, run, loop, branch, remotes, fetch_url, push_url,
#       head, remote_head, pushed, records, pending, state: {next, round, attempt [,
#       refused]}, not_ready, items: [{item, kind, selected}], ledger: [{key, grade,
#       disposition, route}], returned: {first, then}} — `records` is how many commits
#       lie on the opening, `pending` how many lines `git status --porcelain` has,
#       `pushed` whether the bare repository stands at the clone's head, `state` what
#       the run's own state says a `test` stage would work on, `returned` whether each
#       file of `returns` is there.
#       A WRITE OF THE RUN THAT WAS KILLED BETWEEN ITS FILES IS A STATE, AND IS READ AS
#       ONE: the record script does not read the run's state across it (its refusal
#       `interrupted`), and `check` then exits 0 all the same — `state` is {next: null,
#       round: null, attempt: null, refused: interrupted}, after it stands interrupted:
#       {said: that script's own line, then: what follows — the next invocation of the
#       stage finishes the write by itself}, and what the state alone could say is
#       empty or null. NO REFUSAL OF A TOOL `check` CALLS — git, the clone's record
#       script — IS EVER TURNED INTO *the root holds no canary*: such a refusal is
#       `tool`, and its recommendation says that the canary stands as it stood and
#       that no setup is asked for. Only a root with no canary.json holds none (missing)
#       — and a canary.json that lacks what `check` reads the root by is refused by the
#       same word, in a sentence of its own: nothing of a root is read on a guess.
#       FOR A ROOT OF THE PARTS — one a setup under --parts made — the line holds no
#       `returned`, whose two files no act of the parts writes, and holds instead, after
#       `ledger`: applied (below) · budget: {limit, spent, left} · queue: the keys the
#       queue ranks · listed: {WHY: [key]} · human: [{key, why}], the human's list ·
#       filed: [{file, key, verdict, attempt, sha256, counts, why, recorded, handed_on
#       [, applied] [, no_file, set_aside]}] — EVERY FILED VERDICT, with the sha256 of its
#       bytes, whether it counts and whether a record took it: the same file is printed
#       with the same hash before and after that record · view: {file, sha256, on_disk},
#       the stop's view · attempts: {begun, unrecorded} · held (below) · trail: [{commit,
#       subject, attempts, author, committer, canary}] — THE RECORDS MADE, every commit on
#       the opening with the attempts whose marker it took, who made it, and whether author
#       and committer are both the canary's (THE CANARY'S IDENTITY) · identity: {canary,
#       others} — that identity, and every commit of the trail that does not carry it ·
#       pending_paths: what `git status` shows, by its
#       path · acts: [{act, returned, status, next, own, shows, stands}] (below) ·
#       guard (THE WRITE GUARD'S PROBE, below) ·
#       ruling (below) · tool: {wrote, commit, reads, same} — THE THREE TOOLS, by sha256:
#       the one that wrote the opening, the one the clone's commit holds at this tool's
#       path, and the one that asks now; `same` where they are one · sheet: [{plan, key,
#       class, returns}] — THE ANSWER SHEET · to_answer.
#
#       WHAT `check` SAYS OF THE FIELDS THAT ARE READ FROM THE RUN'S TABLES. The run's
#       state is read by the clone's record script from the tables AS THE WORKING TREE
#       HOLDS THEM: `state`, `not_ready`, `items`, `ledger`, `budget`, `queue`, `listed`,
#       `human`, `filed`, `view` and `attempts` are that state's. `head`, `remote_head`,
#       `pushed`, `records`, `trail` and `pending_paths` are git's, and say what is
#       committed. The two differ in ONE state a run reaches: A BATCH THAT IS APPLIED AND
#       IN NO COMMIT — a record whose gate was held when its invocation was ended, or
#       halted. So `check` asks the clone's record script what is pending (`pending`, a
#       read), asks git whether HEAD holds every file of the batch as the batch wrote it
#       (the step tool's own question; its `git-state --look` is not asked — it fetches,
#       and its `git status` may take the index's lock), and prints
#         applied   null where no batch is applied. Else {subject, round, calls, files,
#                   filed, altered, in_commit, then [, across, discard]}: the batch as the
#                   record script says it — its files and the filed verdicts it takes,
#                   by their paths — and WHETHER A COMMIT HOLDS IT. Where none does:
#                   `across`, every field of this line that was read across the batch
#                   and says what the record WILL say; `discard`, the ONE call that takes
#                   the batch back, spelled whole, run from the clone — the exit where a
#                   halt says no name is left for its gate, or the batch is not to be
#                   recorded; and `then`, what follows: the next invocation that does not
#                   only look finishes the batch, whatever it carries. Where the record
#                   script cannot say what is pending — a link among the filed verdicts
#                   is refused there — {refused: its line, then}: no invocation gets
#                   past its first read while that stands.
#       And while a batch is applied and in no commit: `state` holds `across: applied`;
#       A FILED VERDICT THE BATCH TAKES IS PRINTED AS ONE NO RECORD TOOK — `recorded`
#       false, `applied` true: `recorded` is asked of what is committed — and of each act
#       `shows` counts the batch as the record it will be (WHAT `check` SAYS OF EACH ACT).
#       A filed entry that is a link or a directory says so (`no_file`), with the one
#       call that sets it aside (`set_aside`), run from the clone.
#
#         held      THE HELD COMMANDS under the scratch root, [{name, kind, state, why,
#                   verdict, kept [, supervised]}] — EACH AS THE STEP TOOL WOULD ANSWER IT,
#                   read and not asked: that tool has no call that only reads a job (a
#                   wait, and a start asked again, judge a command that has ended and
#                   keep its verdict). `state`: running — its supervisor holds the job's
#                   lock · done — the command has ended, and its supervisor wrote how ·
#                   dead — `why` says how: no-record (the directory holds no record;
#                   `supervised`: whether a supervisor lives under it all the same),
#                   no-exit (the supervisor is gone and wrote nothing), killed (a signal
#                   ended the command) · null — a hand was in the job's directory (why:
#                   no-lock, verdict-unread), and the step tool answers nothing of it.
#                   `verdict`: what the step tool answers now — null while none is kept,
#                   and `void` (why: hash-differs) for a build whose binary is no longer
#                   the file it hashed. `kept`: THE KEPT WORD, as the verdict's file holds
#                   it. WHAT IT CANNOT TELL: whether the command of a job that is `dead`
#                   still runs — a killed supervisor leaves its command running.
#         ruling    null, or THE RULING OFF THE MAIN LINE THAT IS THE NEXT STEP, as the
#                   state says it: {ruling, prompt, withheld, only} — the prompt file to
#                   paste. NEVER NAMED WHILE AN ACT MAY BE RUNNING, as far as `check` can
#                   see one: while a held command's supervisor lives, or a batch is
#                   applied and in no commit, `prompt` is null and `withheld` says why.
#                   `only` says what it cannot see: A SESSION. While an attempt is under
#                   way between two held commands the state reads as it does once that
#                   attempt is dead — so no ruling is typed while a session of an act is
#                   open, whatever this field says.
#
# WHAT THE ORCHESTRATOR INVOKES — the line of `setup`, with nothing to interpret:
#
#   session_cwd   THE DIRECTORY THE INVOKING SESSION STANDS IN: the clone. A stage runs
#                 in whatever repository its session stands in, and every agent's shell
#                 starts there.
#   script_path   the harness, BY ITS PATH INSIDE THE CLONE. The Workflow is invoked by
#                 `scriptPath` and never by its name: a workflow invoked by name runs the
#                 copy the session loaded first, which goes stale without a word.
#   args          the `args` object of the FIRST invocation, as it is: the `test` stage,
#                 the run, the scratch root, and a `scope` that names the round's one door.
#   args_then     the `args` object of EVERY LATER invocation: the same without `scope`.
#                 A round's scope is written once, and an invocation that finishes a
#                 triage takes none — the first arguments, sent again, are refused there.
#   returns       the two files under ROOT/results the session writes what each
#                 invocation returned to, as JSON: `first`, and `then`.
#   check         the one command that reads the result: one plain invocation of this
#                 tool, run from this repository's root.
#   expect        how many agents the first invocation launches where every agent ends
#                 well and leaves nothing open, and how many of those are one-command
#                 steps of dev/stabilize-step — counted, and held to a simulated stage by
#                 this tool's suite; and the plan's estimate of the wall time in minutes
#                 for the five default items, which nobody has measured (null for any
#                 other set).
#
# THE STEPS OF THE SMALL CANARY, each one plain invocation:
#
#   1. A root, outside the repository:
#        mktemp -d /ABSOLUTE/SCRATCH/canary.XXXXXX
#   2. The setup, from this repository's root, with the path step 1 printed:
#        dev/stabilize-canary setup --root ROOT --commit HEAD --run canary-small --previous VERSION --previous-commit SHA
#   3. The session, STANDING IN `session_cwd`, invokes the Workflow with `scriptPath` =
#      `script_path` and `args` = `args`, and writes what it returned to `returns.first`.
#      WHO STARTS THAT SESSION IS NOT DECIDED HERE (the plan leaves it to the human): he
#      starts one in the clone with a prompt the orchestrator hands him — the only kind
#      of session that can stop a subagent from outside — or the orchestrator launches a
#      headless one there. Both need exactly these fields, and nothing else of this tool.
#   4. The same session invokes it once more with `args` = `args_then`, and writes what
#      that returned to `returns.then`.
#   5. The result, read from this repository's root:
#        dev/stabilize-canary check --root ROOT
#      and the two files of `returns`. What it showed is the orchestrator's record,
#      aggregated; nothing of the clone's tree comes back.
#
# THE SYNTHETIC OPENING — never the human-led opening of a run: the smallest test set
# that has one item of each kind a real round 1 has. Everything in it is this tool's
# table (ITEMS, CLAUSES, DOOR, SEEDED), and its text is printable ASCII.
#
#   the census   no-lost-files, working-product, usable-by-agents, migration-works — the
#                exit rule's four clauses (DECISIONS.md -> 2026-10-04). The fourth has no
#                item, as the port rehearsal has no instrument.
#   the stop     every-round, the default scope `delta`, no declared bound
#   the door     ONE: `jigc doc list`. `args.scope` names it, the review row covers it,
#                and the seeded finding stands at it — one text, from one constant.
#   the items    * row-doc-list    review-row           no-lost-files     in scope, by the door
#                * cross-cutting   audit-cross-cutting  no-lost-files     every candidate
#                * arm-control     trial-arm            usable-by-agents  every candidate
#                  drive-doc-list  audit-drive          usable-by-agents  every candidate
#                * gate            held-gate            working-product   every candidate
#                * regression-set  held-regression      working-product   every candidate
#                (*: run where --items names none.) `arm-control` is walk arm 00 of the trial
#                protocol, the positive control: it is scripted, so it needs the trial
#                image and a container runtime and no credential. `drive-doc-list` is ITS
#                STAND-IN for a machine without one. `gate` and `regression-set` are
#                HELD CHECKS: the kind of each names the command the step tool holds
#                (its `hold-start --kind gate`, `--kind regression`); that tool starts
#                it, a step of the stage asks after it, the tool judges it, and the
#                item's result is written from the tool's verdict file and from no
#                agent's word. `gate` is answered from the candidate's own gate, which
#                every stage holds once and does not run twice; `regression-set` is the
#                long scripted check, about thirty-five minutes, started after that gate
#                and never beside it.
#   the finding  ONE ledger row of round 0, `canary-seeded-claim`, ungraded: a CLAIM
#                about the door that would break the first clause if it were true — that
#                `jigc doc list`, at exit 0, removes an untracked file — with its block
#                in the opening record. Nobody is told the answer; triage is handed the
#                row, and whatever it grades `breaks` or `unclear` a verifier re-drives.
#                (Driven once when this tool was built, on 1.0.0-rc.24: the file stood.
#                So `refuted` is what a verifier should return, and `confirmed` would be
#                a real finding about the product.)
#
# THE OPENING OF A RUN IN PARTS — `setup --parts` (the plan for running the reshaped
# `test` stage for real in parts, staged-canary.md beside the reshape plan under
# completions/artifacts/M55/stabilization-build/reshape/: its section 4, and `SC2` of its
# section 10; DECISIONS.md -> 2026-10-12, "The canary's setup opens a run with a queue").
# ONE SWITCH, AND WITHOUT IT A SETUP IS WHAT IT WAS, TO THE BYTE — the suite holds a
# setup without the switch to what this tool made before it had one. Under it the setup
# writes another opening through the same calls of the clone's own record script: A RUN
# WITH A QUEUE, cut down to what the parts run. All of it is this section's tables
# (PARTS_DOOR, PARTS_ITEMS, BUDGET, HAND_ON, ORDER, CONDITION, PLANTS):
#
#   the census   the same four clauses; the stop, the scope and the bounds the same
